1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,840
In 1977, a drought hit Daphne Major, a

2
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:06,080
tiny island in the Galápagos.

3
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:08,800
The small, soft seeds ran out, and most

4
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:11,280
of the medium ground finches died.

5
00:00:11,320 --> 00:00:14,320
The survivors tended to have deeper beaks, strong

6
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:16,560
enough to crack the big, hard seeds that

7
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:17,680
were left.

8
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:20,640
Beak depth is largely inherited, so their chicks

9
00:00:20,747 --> 00:00:23,120
hatched with deeper beaks on average, too.

10
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:25,920
No new mutation was needed, because the variation

11
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:28,160
in beaks was already there.

12
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:30,240
So where does all the variation in each

13
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:32,000
new generation come from?

14
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:35,760
Three processes produce it, and you've met them

15
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:36,320
all.

16
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:39,360
A mutation changes the base sequence, so it's

17
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:41,440
the only one that can make a brand-new

18
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:42,000
allele.

19
00:00:42,560 --> 00:00:45,680
Meiosis shuffles the alleles a parent already has

20
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:48,560
into gametes that differ, by crossing over and

21
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:50,320
independent assortment.

22
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:53,560
And fertilisation joins one gamete from each parent,

23
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:54,800
at random.

24
00:00:54,880 --> 00:00:56,960
So how do the three fit together?

25
00:00:57,040 --> 00:00:59,280
Let's follow one new allele, from the moment

26
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:00,160
it appears.

27
00:01:01,367 --> 00:01:04,086
Suppose one sperm carries a mutation in a

28
00:01:04,167 --> 00:01:06,726
beak gene, and it fertilises an egg.

29
00:01:07,047 --> 00:01:09,126
The chick that grows has the new allele

30
00:01:09,207 --> 00:01:12,246
in every cell, because every cell is copied

31
00:01:12,327 --> 00:01:13,606
from that first one.

32
00:01:13,927 --> 00:01:16,406
It sits on one chromosome of a pair,

33
00:01:16,487 --> 00:01:19,046
with the original allele on the partner chromosome.

34
00:01:19,767 --> 00:01:22,566
When this bird makes its own gametes, meiosis

35
00:01:22,647 --> 00:01:24,326
one separates the partners.

36
00:01:24,807 --> 00:01:26,646
So about half its gametes get the new

37
00:01:26,727 --> 00:01:28,966
allele, and half get the original.

38
00:01:30,167 --> 00:01:33,206
Meiosis doesn't just pass the new allele on,

39
00:01:33,287 --> 00:01:35,086
it gives it new neighbours.

40
00:01:35,367 --> 00:01:39,446
In crossing over, partner chromosomes swap matching pieces,

41
00:01:39,527 --> 00:01:41,286
so the new allele can end up beside

42
00:01:41,367 --> 00:01:43,366
different alleles of nearby genes.

43
00:01:44,007 --> 00:01:46,966
Independent assortment then sends its chromosome into a

44
00:01:47,047 --> 00:01:49,766
gamete with either partner of every other pair.

45
00:01:50,327 --> 00:01:52,646
So one bird spreads its new allele across

46
00:01:52,807 --> 00:01:55,846
many combinations, and none of that makes another

47
00:01:55,927 --> 00:01:56,726
new allele.

48
00:01:57,934 --> 00:02:00,893
Then fertilisation joins each gamete with one from

49
00:02:00,974 --> 00:02:01,773
another bird.

50
00:02:02,254 --> 00:02:03,933
If the carrier mates with a bird that

51
00:02:04,014 --> 00:02:06,413
lacks the new allele, no chick can get

52
00:02:06,494 --> 00:02:09,133
two copies, but the allele spreads to some

53
00:02:09,174 --> 00:02:10,493
of its chicks.

54
00:02:10,814 --> 00:02:13,133
Later, when two of those carriers mate, a

55
00:02:13,214 --> 00:02:15,453
chick can inherit two copies, with a one

56
00:02:15,534 --> 00:02:17,773
in four chance each time.

57
00:02:17,934 --> 00:02:20,413
So if the new allele is recessive, its

58
00:02:20,614 --> 00:02:24,253
effect stays hidden until two carriers' gametes meet.

59
00:02:24,294 --> 00:02:27,293
Fertilisation has made a genotype that never existed

60
00:02:27,654 --> 00:02:29,533
before, from alleles that did.

61
00:02:30,734 --> 00:02:33,213
So how much does each process add in

62
00:02:33,294 --> 00:02:34,453
one generation?

63
00:02:34,734 --> 00:02:37,533
In humans, scientists have counted by reading the

64
00:02:37,614 --> 00:02:39,613
DNA of parents and their children.

65
00:02:40,174 --> 00:02:43,453
Each child carries, on average, about sixty to

66
00:02:43,494 --> 00:02:46,813
seventy new mutations, out of roughly six billion

67
00:02:46,894 --> 00:02:47,533
bases.

68
00:02:48,174 --> 00:02:51,453
Meanwhile, each parent has two different versions at

69
00:02:51,614 --> 00:02:53,613
millions of places in their DNA.

70
00:02:54,254 --> 00:02:56,413
Meiosis can sort them into more than eight

71
00:02:56,654 --> 00:03:00,893
million chromosome combinations, before crossing over adds more,

72
00:03:00,934 --> 00:03:04,693
and fertilisation multiplies the two parents' choices together.

73
00:03:05,054 --> 00:03:08,013
So when two siblings differ, almost all of

74
00:03:08,134 --> 00:03:11,613
those differences were already in their parents, reshuffled.

75
00:03:12,834 --> 00:03:16,113
But remember from Module five: every allele being

76
00:03:16,194 --> 00:03:19,473
reshuffled first appeared as a mutation, at some

77
00:03:19,554 --> 00:03:21,393
time, in some ancestor.

78
00:03:22,034 --> 00:03:24,433
That's why mutation is called the ultimate source

79
00:03:24,514 --> 00:03:28,113
of variation, and meiosis and fertilisation the immediate

80
00:03:28,194 --> 00:03:28,673
source.

81
00:03:29,314 --> 00:03:32,673
If mutation stopped, meiosis and fertilisation would keep

82
00:03:32,754 --> 00:03:35,553
dealing new combinations, but from a fixed set

83
00:03:35,594 --> 00:03:36,273
of alleles.

84
00:03:36,914 --> 00:03:39,633
Any allele the population lost, by chance or

85
00:03:39,914 --> 00:03:41,953
selection, would never be made again.

86
00:03:42,834 --> 00:03:45,393
The finches' beak alleles were made by mutations

87
00:03:45,554 --> 00:03:46,833
long before the drought.

88
00:03:48,034 --> 00:03:51,153
What about organisms that reproduce without meiosis or

89
00:03:51,234 --> 00:03:52,113
fertilisation?

90
00:03:52,754 --> 00:03:55,553
A bacterium copies its DNA and splits in

91
00:03:55,634 --> 00:03:58,913
two, so its offspring are clones, except for

92
00:03:58,994 --> 00:04:00,193
any new mutations.

93
00:04:00,834 --> 00:04:04,113
With huge numbers and fast division, mutation does

94
00:04:04,274 --> 00:04:05,553
much more of the work here.

95
00:04:06,274 --> 00:04:08,593
Bacteria can also pick up genes from other

96
00:04:08,674 --> 00:04:11,073
bacteria, for example on plasmids.

97
00:04:11,714 --> 00:04:15,713
So in bacteria, which reproduce asexually, new variation

98
00:04:15,794 --> 00:04:19,073
comes from mutation and gene transfer, not from

99
00:04:19,154 --> 00:04:20,913
meiosis and fertilisation.

100
00:04:22,134 --> 00:04:23,733
Here's a way to hold it, with the

101
00:04:23,854 --> 00:04:25,653
deck of cards from Module five.

102
00:04:26,134 --> 00:04:28,053
Mutation prints a new card.

103
00:04:28,534 --> 00:04:31,653
Meiosis deals each gamete a different hand, and

104
00:04:31,734 --> 00:04:33,973
fertilisation puts two hands together.

105
00:04:34,614 --> 00:04:37,653
In one generation, almost all the variety comes

106
00:04:37,734 --> 00:04:38,533
from the dealing.

107
00:04:38,934 --> 00:04:41,413
But over a long time, every card was

108
00:04:41,494 --> 00:04:42,773
once printed new.

109
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:45,440
Two exam questions now.

110
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:46,720
First.

111
00:04:47,040 --> 00:04:49,680
A mutation in one sperm creates a new

112
00:04:49,760 --> 00:04:52,480
recessive allele, n, of a gene on a

113
00:04:52,600 --> 00:04:53,520
numbered chromosome.

114
00:04:54,240 --> 00:04:57,040
That sperm fertilises an egg, and the animal

115
00:04:57,120 --> 00:04:59,440
that develops has one copy of n in

116
00:04:59,520 --> 00:05:00,800
every cell.

117
00:05:00,880 --> 00:05:02,640
It later mates with an animal that has

118
00:05:02,760 --> 00:05:04,040
no copies of n.

119
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:06,800
What fraction of their offspring would you expect

120
00:05:06,880 --> 00:05:08,400
to carry n?

121
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:11,040
Then explain how an animal with two copies

122
00:05:11,120 --> 00:05:14,000
of n could arise in a later generation.

123
00:05:14,080 --> 00:05:15,360
Pause here to answer.

124
00:05:25,767 --> 00:05:27,686
The carrier is N n.

125
00:05:27,767 --> 00:05:31,926
Meiosis separates its two partner chromosomes, so half

126
00:05:32,007 --> 00:05:33,846
its gametes carry n.

127
00:05:33,967 --> 00:05:36,726
So about half the offspring carry one copy,

128
00:05:36,807 --> 00:05:38,566
and none has two.

129
00:05:38,767 --> 00:05:42,086
Later, if two carriers mate, each passes n

130
00:05:42,207 --> 00:05:45,366
in half its gametes, and fertilisation joins two

131
00:05:45,527 --> 00:05:48,726
n gametes in about one offspring in four.

132
00:05:48,767 --> 00:05:51,766
Saying every offspring inherits n, because the parent

133
00:05:51,847 --> 00:05:54,006
has it, is the classic mistake.

134
00:05:55,234 --> 00:05:56,193
Second.

135
00:05:56,234 --> 00:06:00,273
A student claims, "Meiosis and fertilisation produce almost

136
00:06:00,434 --> 00:06:03,553
all the genetic variation in each generation, so

137
00:06:03,714 --> 00:06:07,633
mutation isn't important." Evaluate this claim.

138
00:06:07,794 --> 00:06:09,073
Pause here to answer.

139
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:21,020
The first part is right.

140
00:06:21,260 --> 00:06:24,220
In one generation, nearly all the differences between

141
00:06:24,300 --> 00:06:26,340
offspring come from reshuffling.

142
00:06:26,620 --> 00:06:28,540
But the conclusion is wrong.

143
00:06:28,860 --> 00:06:32,700
Reshuffling only recombines alleles that mutation made, so

144
00:06:33,060 --> 00:06:35,660
mutation is the ultimate source, and it matters

145
00:06:35,740 --> 00:06:38,340
even more in asexual organisms.

146
00:06:38,700 --> 00:06:40,620
So the claim is partly correct.

147
00:06:40,860 --> 00:06:43,260
Saying crossing over makes new alleles is the

148
00:06:43,340 --> 00:06:44,540
classic mistake.

149
00:06:45,767 --> 00:06:48,326
So when a question asks where variation came

150
00:06:48,487 --> 00:06:50,806
from, sort each change.

151
00:06:51,047 --> 00:06:52,406
Is it a brand-new allele?

152
00:06:52,727 --> 00:06:53,886
That's mutation.

153
00:06:54,167 --> 00:06:56,886
Is it a new combination of existing alleles?

154
00:06:57,127 --> 00:07:00,086
Then say which process: crossing over within a

155
00:07:00,207 --> 00:07:04,966
chromosome, independent assortment between chromosomes, or fertilisation

156
00:07:05,047 --> 00:07:06,726
joining two parents' gametes.

157
00:07:07,287 --> 00:07:10,886
Next, how mutation, gene flow and drift change

158
00:07:10,927 --> 00:07:13,046
the alleles of a whole population.

159
00:07:14,267 --> 00:07:15,706
Here are the key notes.

160
00:07:15,947 --> 00:07:17,866
Pause the video if you need more time

161
00:07:17,947 --> 00:07:18,746
to copy them.
